{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "prop:separable-witness",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For constants whose bounds contain the displayed Jacobian ranges and for \\(\\beta\\) below the displayed displacement and cut-locus margins, the construction in def:separable-witness satisfies \\(P^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\in\\mathcal F_s\\). The translation coordinates in the three triples returned by \\(\\operatorname{Lift}_{\\mathbb T}\\) are zero; the potential coordinates for \\(h_1^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\) and \\(h_{1\\star}^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\) are respectively \\(-0.1\\cos(2\\pi u_1)/(2\\pi^2)\\) and \\(-0.15\\cos(2\\pi u_1)/(2\\pi^2)+0.05\\cos(2\\pi u_2)/(2\\pi^2)\\). At \\(u=(1/4,1/4)\\) and \\(y=h_{1\\star}^{\\mathrm{sep}}(u)\\), its translation-aware displacement is \\(\\operatorname{Disp}_{\\mathbb T}(T,y)=(0.05/\\pi,-0.05/\\pi)\\neq0\\), its outer Jacobian is positive definite, and equal cell sizes give \\(V_j=I_2/\\pi\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(P^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal F_s\\)",
      "\\(h_{1\\star}^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\)",
      "\\(h_0^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\)",
      "\\(h_1^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\)",
      "\\(\\operatorname{Lift}_{\\mathbb T}\\)",
      "\\(\\beta\\)",
      "\\(T\\)",
      "\\(\\operatorname{Disp}_{\\mathbb T}\\)",
      "\\(V(P)\\)",
      "\\(j\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:identified-functional",
      "def:model-class",
      "def:separable-witness",
      "ass:lift-regularity",
      "lem:full-critical-covariance",
      "lem:torus-cic-identification",
      "ass:cic-identification"
    ],
    "justification": "The witness proves nonemptiness with a nonzero causal arrow and avoids relying only on the identity-map law.",
    "gap": "The available uniform-law calculation has zero displacement; this witness keeps the critical constant while producing a genuine bivariate effect.",
    "consumer": "The witness supplies the first reproducible analytic benchmark for the proposed nonseparable numerical kill test and for software validation.",
    "proof_tex": "The Jacobians of the three production maps at \\(u\\) are\n\\[\nDh_0^{\\mathrm{sep}}=I_2,\n\\quad Dh_1^{\\mathrm{sep}}=\\operatorname{diag}(1+0.2\\cos(2\\pi u_1),1),\n\\]\n\\[\nDh_{1\\star}^{\\mathrm{sep}}\n=\\operatorname{diag}(1+0.3\\cos(2\\pi u_1),1-0.1\\cos(2\\pi u_2)).\n\\]\nTheir eigenvalues lie respectively in \\([1,1]\\), \\([0.8,1.2]\\), and \\([0.7,1.3]\\); hence all three maps are periodic diffeomorphisms.  Their displayed lifts are gradients of strictly convex Euclidean lifts.  The corresponding periodic potentials have zero mean and equal\n\\[\n\\phi_{h_1}(u)=-\\frac{0.1}{2\\pi^2}\\cos(2\\pi u_1),\n\\qquad\n\\phi_{h_{1\\star}}(u)=-\\frac{0.15}{2\\pi^2}\\cos(2\\pi u_1)\n+\\frac{0.05}{2\\pi^2}\\cos(2\\pi u_2).\n\\]\nThus their selected translations are zero.\n\nSince \\(h_0\\) is the identity and both latent laws are uniform,\n\\(f_{C0}=f_{T0}=1\\), \\(p=h_1^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\), and\n\\(T=h_1^{\\mathrm{sep}}\\circ(h_{1\\star}^{\\mathrm{sep}})^{-1}\\).  At \\(y=h_{1\\star}^{\\mathrm{sep}}(u)\\),\n\\[\nH(y)=\\operatorname{diag}\\left(\n\\frac{1+0.2\\cos(2\\pi u_1)}{1+0.3\\cos(2\\pi u_1)},\n\\frac1{1-0.1\\cos(2\\pi u_2)}\\right)\\succ0.\n\\]\nThe first eigenvalue lies in \\([12/13,8/7]\\) and the second in \\([10/11,10/9]\\).  The pushforward densities lie between the reciprocals of the displayed positive Jacobian determinants; in particular\n\\(100/143\\leq f_{T1}\\leq100/63\\).  All functions are smooth.  Choosing the class constants to contain these ranges and choosing \\(\\beta\\) below the positive displacement and cut margins proves membership in \\(\\mathcal F_s\\).  The maps \\(p\\) and \\(T\\) have positive symmetric Jacobians, so their equivariant Euclidean lifts are gradients of convex functions.  Fenchel's inequality applied to every lattice translate gives torus dual potentials attaining equality on their graphs; hence those graphs are \\(c_{\\mathbb T}\\)-cyclically monotone and ass:cic-identification is met.\n\nAt \\(u=(1/4,1/4)\\), all cosines vanish and\n\\[\ny-T(y)=h_{1\\star}^{\\mathrm{sep}}(u)-h_1^{\\mathrm{sep}}(u)\n=\\left(\\frac{0.05}{\\pi},-\\frac{0.05}{\\pi}\\right)\\neq0.\n\\]\nAt the same point \\(H(y)=I_2\\), \\(f_{T1}(y)=1\\), and \\(\\alpha(y)=1\\).  Equal cell sizes give \\(w_j=4\\), and lem:full-critical-covariance yields \\(V_j=I_2/\\pi\\), as claimed.",
